          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What distinguishes compound lipids from simple lipids?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What distinguishes compound lipids from simple lipids?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>They are always liquid at room temperature</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Whether a lipid is solid or liquid depends on fatty acid saturation, not its classification category. Saturated fats are solid; unsaturated fats are liquid.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>They carry an additional chemical group such as phosphate, sugar, or protein</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct — phospholipids carry phosphate, glycolipids carry sugar, and lipoproteins carry protein, making compound lipids structurally and functionally distinct from simple lipids.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>They are produced by hydrolysis of other lipid classes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Lipids produced by hydrolysis are classified as derived lipids — examples include fatty acids, cholesterol, and prostaglandins.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>They provide more energy per gram than simple lipids</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Triglycerides, which are simple lipids, are the body's primary energy store at 9 kcal per gram. Compound lipids serve structural and transport roles rather than energy storage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Compound lipids carry an additional chemical group beyond the basic fatty acid–alcohol ester: phosphate in phospholipids, sugar in glycolipids, and protein in lipoproteins."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Compound lipids are defined by an additional chemical group — phosphate, sugar, or protein — which gives them specialised roles in membranes, signalling, and lipid transport."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
