          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which molecule inhibits carnitine palmitoyltransferase-1 (CPT-1), preventing fat</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which molecule inhibits carnitine palmitoyltransferase-1 (CPT-1), preventing fatty acid synthesis and breakdown from running simultaneously?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Acetyl-CoA</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Acetyl-CoA is the substrate that gets activated and enters the carnitine shuttle. It does not regulate CPT-1 directly.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Citrate</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Citrate shuttles acetyl-CoA out of the mitochondria for use in lipogenesis, but it is not the molecule that inhibits CPT-1.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Malonyl-CoA</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct — malonyl-CoA is the first committed intermediate of fatty acid synthesis. Its presence signals that lipogenesis is active and blocks CPT-1, preventing simultaneous fat breakdown.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>FADH₂</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>FADH₂ is a product of the first oxidation step in each beta-oxidation cycle. It is an electron carrier, not a regulator of CPT-1.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The answer is malonyl-CoA. It is the first committed intermediate of lipogenesis and inhibits CPT-1, ensuring that fatty acid synthesis and beta-oxidation do not run at the same time."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Malonyl-CoA acts as a metabolic switch — when lipogenesis is active, malonyl-CoA rises and inhibits CPT-1, blocking fatty acid entry into the mitochondria for oxidation."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
