          "label": "<p>How does SREBP (sterol regulatory element-binding protein) respond when intracel</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>How does SREBP (sterol regulatory element-binding protein) respond when intracellular cholesterol levels are low?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>It inhibits HMG-CoA reductase to conserve remaining cholesterol</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>SREBP does the opposite — it activates HMG-CoA reductase gene expression when cholesterol is low, increasing synthesis to replenish levels.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It moves to the nucleus and activates genes for HMG-CoA reductase and LDL receptors</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct — when cholesterol drops, SREBP is released from the ER membrane, enters the nucleus, and switches on genes for both cholesterol synthesis (HMG-CoA reductase) and cholesterol uptake from blood (LDL receptors).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It stimulates conversion of cholesterol to bile acids for enterohepatic recycling</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bile acid synthesis is the main elimination route for cholesterol, but SREBP's role is to increase cholesterol levels when they are low, not to promote elimination.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It remains anchored at the endoplasmic reticulum membrane and blocks transcription</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This describes what happens when cholesterol is high, not low. When cholesterol is abundant, SREBP stays at the ER and transcription of cholesterol-related genes stops.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "When cholesterol is low, SREBP moves from the ER membrane to the nucleus, activating transcription of HMG-CoA reductase and LDL receptor genes. When cholesterol is high, SREBP stays anchored at the ER."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "SREBP is a cholesterol sensor — when levels are low, it translocates to the nucleus and upregulates both HMG-CoA reductase (more synthesis) and LDL receptors (more uptake from blood)."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
