          "label": "<p>Which process carried out by high-density lipoprotein (HDL) explains why high HD</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which process carried out by high-density lipoprotein (HDL) explains why high HDL levels are protective against cardiovascular disease?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Delivering cholesterol to peripheral tissues for membrane synthesis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cholesterol delivery to peripheral tissues is the function of LDL, not HDL. This outward transport is why elevated LDL is harmful.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Converting VLDL to LDL through progressive triglyceride removal</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>VLDL-to-LDL conversion is carried out by lipoprotein lipase and hepatic lipase acting on VLDL particles, not by HDL.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Collecting excess cholesterol from peripheral tissues and returning it to the liver</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct — this process is called reverse cholesterol transport. HDL removes excess cholesterol from tissues, including artery walls, and delivers it to the liver for excretion as bile acids.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Directly inhibiting oxidation of LDL particles within the arterial wall</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While HDL has some antioxidant properties, its primary cardioprotective mechanism is reverse cholesterol transport, not direct inhibition of LDL oxidation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "HDL protects against cardiovascular disease through reverse cholesterol transport: it collects excess cholesterol from tissues and delivers it to the liver for disposal."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "HDL performs reverse cholesterol transport — collecting excess cholesterol from peripheral tissues and returning it to the liver, where it can be excreted as bile acids."
                }
              ],
